    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d583">

                <pb facs="0847" n="833" sameAs="frus:frus1947v05/pg833"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.BC Greece/4–1447: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to Mr. Mark F. Ethridge, at Geneva</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-04-14T19:00:00-05:00">April 14, 1947—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>139. For Ethridge. Dept has reconsidered Deptel 200<anchor n="1" xml:id="d583-f1"/> to Athens in light of
                    your No. 34<anchor n="2" xml:id="d583-f2"/> and of SC discussions, especially with respect <pb facs="0848" n="834"
                        xml:id="pg834"/> to US aid program. We hope that Commission’s report will be
                    considered by SC prior to any SC action re aid program.</p>
                <p>We agree with your No. 34 that continuing Commission is likely to be more
                    effective if there are no stringent limitations on its authority as mediator of
                    first resort. We also agree that it might appropriately include such questions
                    as Salonika,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d583-f3"/> refugees and hostages. However, we
                    do not wish to broaden terms of reference in such manner that any member of
                    continuing Commission could use Commission as a device to delay or forestall US
                    aid program or to use latter as pretext for postponing legitimate business of
                    Commission.</p>
                <p>For your info US will probably oppose any attempt in SC to link functions of continuing Commission to any proposal for
                    UN supervision or observation of US aid program.</p>
                <p>Dept’s views re neutral observers set forth its 140, 7 p. m. today.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d583-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent Geneva. Repeated Athens, Salonika, Belgrade, Sofia, New York, London, Moscow
                    for the Secretary.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d583-f1" n="1" xml:id="d583-f1-content">
                <p>Dated February 20, <ref target="#pg821">p. 821</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f2" n="2" xml:id="d583-f2-content">
                <p>Dated April 1, not printed; but see telegram 89, April 3, to Geneva, and footnote
                    1, <ref target="#pg830">p. 830</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f3" n="3" xml:id="d583-f3-content">
                <p>On April 1, the Department informed Mr. Ethridge in telegram 186, to Belgrade,
                    that “it is public knowledge that US favors an international free zone at
                    Salonika under Greek sovereignty and administration.” It stated further that the
                    establishment of such a zone should be negotiated by the Greek, Yugoslav and
                    Bulgarian Governments after the solution of the present border disturbances
                    (501.BC Greece/4–147).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f4" n="4" xml:id="d583-f4-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
